<div class="box">
  <h3>Child Status Sync Plugin Settings</h3>
  
  <p>
    <label>
      <%= check_box_tag 'settings[enabled]', '1', @settings['enabled'] == '1' %>
      <%= label_tag 'settings[enabled]', 'Enable Child Status Sync' %>
    </label>
    <em style="display: block; margin-top: 5px; color: #666;">When enabled, child issue status changes will automatically update parent issue status</em>
  </p>

  <p>
    <label>
      <%= check_box_tag 'settings[enable_logging]', '1', @settings['enable_logging'] == '1' %>
      <%= label_tag 'settings[enable_logging]', 'Enable Debug Logging' %>
    </label>
    <em style="display: block; margin-top: 5px; color: #666;">When enabled, detailed debug messages will be logged to the Redmine log file for troubleshooting</em>
  </p>

  <p>
    <label for="settings_restricted_trackers">Restricted Trackers (no parent status update)</label><br>
    <%= text_field_tag 'settings[restricted_trackers]', @settings['restricted_trackers'], size: 60 %>
    <em style="display: block; margin-top: 5px; color: #666;">
      Comma-separated tracker names, e.g. <code>Bug,CR_Bug</code>. When a child issue's tracker matches one of these
      names, its status changes will <strong>not</strong> be propagated to any parent issue. Use this to keep Bug /
      CR_Bug from auto-updating their Task, User Story, or CR parents, while the normal
      Task &rarr; User Story &rarr; CR flow keeps syncing normally.
    </em>
  </p>

  <p>
    <label for="settings_status_order">Status Flow Order (one status name per line, earliest first)</label><br>
    <%= text_area_tag 'settings[status_order]', @settings['status_order'], rows: 22, cols: 60, style: 'font-family: monospace;' %>
    <em style="display: block; margin-top: 5px; color: #666;">
      Defines the order the workflow moves through. A child issue's new status will only be pushed onto a parent
      when it is <strong>ahead</strong> of the parent's current status in this list &mdash; a child moving to an
      earlier/previous status in the list will never move a parent backwards.
      If a new status is added to the workflow later, add its name on its own line here (in the right position)
      so it gets picked up &mdash; no code change needed. A status missing from this list is treated as unordered
      and its sync will not be blocked by ordering.
    </em>
  </p>

  <p>
    <label for="settings_restricted_statuses">Restricted Statuses (never propagate to parent)</label><br>
    <%= text_field_tag 'settings[restricted_statuses]', @settings['restricted_statuses'], size: 60 %>
    <em style="display: block; margin-top: 5px; color: #666;">
      Comma-separated status names, e.g. <code>OnHold,Completed,Closed,Cancelled</code>. When a child issue changes
      to one of these statuses, that change is <strong>never</strong> propagated to any parent issue &mdash;
      regardless of tracker or status order. Add any new terminal/paused status here if it should behave the same way.
    </em>
  </p>

  <p style="margin-top: 20px; padding: 10px; background-color: #f5f5f5; border-left: 3px solid #2196F3;">
    <strong>How it works:</strong><br>
    When a child issue's status changes, this plugin updates each parent issue's status to match, unless any of the
    following apply:
    <ul style="margin: 5px 0 0 20px;">
      <li>the child issue's tracker is listed under "Restricted Trackers" (e.g. Bug, CR_Bug never update parents)</li>
      <li>the child's new status is listed under "Restricted Statuses" (e.g. OnHold, Completed, Closed, Cancelled never update parents)</li>
      <li>the child's new status is not ahead of the parent's current status in the "Status Flow Order" (parents are never moved backwards)</li>
    </ul>
    This keeps auto status sync on the normal Task &rarr; User Story &rarr; CR forward-moving flow.
  </p>

  <h3 style="margin-top: 30px;">Auto-Update Parent Dates</h3>

  <p>
    <label for="settings_date_sync_child_trackers">Child Task Trackers (push dates to their parents)</label><br>
    <%= text_field_tag 'settings[date_sync_child_trackers]', @settings['date_sync_child_trackers'], size: 60 %>
    <em style="display: block; margin-top: 5px; color: #666;">
      Comma-separated tracker names, e.g. <code>Task</code>. Only issues of these trackers count as "child tasks" for
      the date sync below &mdash; saving an issue of any other tracker (e.g. Bug/CR_Bug) never affects a parent's dates.
      This is the only tracker-related setting for date sync: whichever trackers are listed here are the ones whose
      date updates get pushed to their parents.
    </em>
  </p>

  <p>
    <label for="settings_date_sync_custom_fields">Date Custom Fields (synced from the latest task)</label><br>
    <%= text_field_tag 'settings[date_sync_custom_fields]', @settings['date_sync_custom_fields'], size: 60 %>
    <em style="display: block; margin-top: 5px; color: #666;">
      Comma-separated custom field names, e.g. <code>Actual start date,Actual end date</code>. Whenever a child task
      is created or updated, each of these fields is copied from it onto every ancestor issue &mdash; but only when
      that child is the <strong>most recently created</strong> eligible task anywhere in that ancestor's subtree (see
      "How it works" below). Add a new field name here (matching an existing custom field exactly) to bring it into
      this flow &mdash; no code change needed.
    </em>
    <em style="display: block; margin-top: 5px; color: #888; font-style: italic;">
      Available issue custom fields (copy the exact spelling from here):
      <%= IssueCustomField.order(:name).pluck(:name).join(', ').presence || 'none defined yet' %>
    </em>
  </p>

  <p>
    <label>
      <%= check_box_tag 'settings[sync_planned_start_date]', '1', @settings['sync_planned_start_date'] != '0' %>
      <%= label_tag 'settings[sync_planned_start_date]', 'Sync Planned Start Date (built-in Start date field)' %>
    </label>
    <em style="display: block; margin-top: 5px; color: #666;">
      When enabled, the built-in Start date is copied the same way as the custom fields above. This is a fixed
      Redmine field, so unlike the custom field list above there is nothing to name here &mdash; just this on/off switch.
    </em>
  </p>

  <p>
    <label>
      <%= check_box_tag 'settings[sync_planned_end_date]', '1', @settings['sync_planned_end_date'] != '0' %>
      <%= label_tag 'settings[sync_planned_end_date]', 'Sync Planned End Date (built-in Due date field)' %>
    </label>
    <em style="display: block; margin-top: 5px; color: #666;">
      Same as "Sync Planned Start Date" above, but for the built-in Due date field.
    </em>
  </p>

  <p style="margin-top: 20px; padding: 10px; background-color: #f5f5f5; border-left: 3px solid #2196F3;">
    <strong>How it works:</strong><br>
    Whenever a child task (tracker listed under "Child Task Trackers", by default Task) is created or updated, this
    plugin looks at <strong>every</strong> eligible task anywhere in each ancestor's subtree (not just direct
    children) and finds whichever one was <strong>created most recently</strong>. Only that one task's dates are
    copied onto the ancestor &mdash; editing any other, earlier-created task never touches that ancestor's dates,
    no matter how many times it's saved afterwards.
    <br><br>
    Example: <code>Change Request &rarr; User Story &rarr; Task1, Task2</code> (Task2 created after Task1).
    Saving Task2 pushes its dates to both User Story and Change Request, since Task2 is the most recently created
    task under both of them. Saving Task1 afterwards does nothing at all to either parent &mdash; Task2 remains the
    latest-created task regardless of which one gets edited, so the dates stay exactly as Task2 last set them.
    This applies the same way across every project and every level of the parent chain.
  </p>
</div>
